  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Anomalously warm temperatures today could \napproach daily record highs.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nThe forecast area will remain in the warm sector of the\napproaching low pressure system today, with a final day of warm\nair advection into the CWA. High temperatures today will lift \nto at least 10 degrees above average, however increasing cloud \ncover ahead of the approaching low pressure system and \ndecreasing wind/advection strength may lead to temperatures not\nquite as warm as earlier in the week, limiting but not \ncompletely eliminating the chance for reaching daily record high\ntemperatures.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Additional rainfall expected today into the \nweekend, though amounts are still uncertain. The rainfall could \nhelp provide some drought relief.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA low pressure system from the Great Lakes region tracks E today.\nThis system helps develop a coastal low system, that will move \nNE along the New England coast. As of this update, models seem \nto agree that a slow frontal system will move along the region\ntoday. Then as the low merges and intensifies, rain amounts \nincrease across the south.\n\nBy Saturday, the low moves NE, spreading rain across the \nregion. Models continue to agree that the low pressure system\nwill slide along the Downeast coast. How this low pressure \ninteracts with the upper-low to the west, however, will \ndetermine how long showers will linger in the region Sunday into\nMonday. GFS is showing the low going through the Bay of Fundy, \ncontinuing into Price Edward Island. The ECMWF, however, shows \nthe low go through the Bay of Fundy, and swing into New \nBrunswick. As of this update, following a track bringing the \nsystem along the coast, into Bay of Fundy and Canada, looking at\na chance for 2.0-3.0 inches along the immediate coast, 1.0-2.0 \ninches in the interior Downeast, 0.5-1 inches in the Central \nHighlands and southern Aroostook, and 0.1-0.5 in northern \nAroostook. Some breezy winds during the day with this system as\nwell.",
